#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""memo-calibrate-drift — measure what the 0.999 provenance threshold must clear.

`memo-verify-provenance` compares a freshly-embedded chunk against the vector on
disk and passes above 0.999. That threshold is only defensible if someone has
measured how far apart those two can drift when NOTHING is wrong. This measures
it, on this corpus against this endpoint.

⭐ **Re-run this after any GPU, driver, vLLM, or model-serving change.** The floor
is set by how much the serving environment has changed since the vectors were
written, and that grows on its own with no event to mark it — a provenance
threshold decays exactly like a provenance record does. Nothing will fail loudly
when the margin erodes; the first symptom is a false PROVENANCE MISMATCH on a
healthy corpus.

Four quantities, one of which is a control:

  A. composition  cos(singleton_i, batch16_i)   — does call shape move a vector?
  B. drift-now    cos(singleton_i, stored_i)    — how far has NOW moved from WRITE?
  C. drift-batch  cos(batch16_i,   stored_i)    — same, at production call shape
  N. control      cos(singleton_0, singleton_k) — two UNRELATED chunks. Must land
                                                  far from 1.0. If it does not,
                                                  the comparison is broken and
                                                  A/B/C are all meaningless.

**A and C exist to separate two things that get conflated.** If B is large you
cannot tell, from B alone, whether the fresh vector differs because it was
computed as a singleton (call shape) or because the serving environment moved
(write-time drift). C holds call shape fixed at what the corpus was actually
written with, so C-vs-B isolates it.

⭐ **WHY IT REPEATS, AND WHY YOU MUST NOT READ ONE RUN.** These quantities are not
stable at n=16. Two back-to-back runs on 2026-08-02 (same corpus, same endpoint,
minutes apart) disagreed on *which* rows deviate and on whether call shape
mattered at all:

    run 1   A 8.66e-05   B 1.15e-04   C 1.15e-04    B == C on every row
    run 2   A 1.71e-04   B 1.59e-04   C 1.15e-04    B != C; A was the LARGEST

Most rows return exactly 1.0 in all three columns; the rest deviate at ~1e-4, and
part of which ones move is a lottery. **So a single run can report A = 0.000000
and conclude "call shape does nothing" — and a second run refutes it.** That is
almost certainly how two careful seats measured this quantity on the same class of
stack and got answers three orders of magnitude apart. Hence `--repeat`: the
reported floor is the WORST across repeats, and the per-round line plus the moved
rows are printed so instability is visible rather than averaged away — it is the
repetition, not the sample size, that separates stable drift from per-call noise.

== BASELINE MEASURED 2026-08-02 02:55–03:10 EDT ==
qwen3-embedding-4b @2560, self-hosted vLLM at 192.168.1.5:31536, n=16 stride 1222
over 19,554 chunks, under GPU contention (a concurrent 7,221-query census plus
another seat's long run). Worst across 3 rounds × 16 chunks:

    A composition   1.18e-04
    B drift-now     1.18e-04
    C drift-batch   1.15e-04
    control         0.3468 – 0.3473

**0.999 clears the worst by 8.5×.** With three rounds the per-row detail separates
two components that a single round conflates:

  · **STABLE** — 4 of the 16 chunks differed from stored in EVERY round, at
    byte-identical values each time (0.99994138, 0.99990418, 0.99988527,
    0.99990468), and identically whether re-embedded singleton or batched. Nothing
    about the call moves these: the endpoint simply does not reproduce today what
    it wrote for those chunks. This is write-time-vs-now drift, and it is the
    component that grows silently.
  · **SPORADIC** — which *additional* row moves varies per round (5, then 7, then
    4 rows of 16). On these, A ≠ 1 while C = 1.0: the singleton call differed and
    the batched one happened to land exactly on the stored value. Per-call
    nondeterminism, not a property of call shape.

Both components sit at ~1e-4. **What cannot be claimed from this data is that
call shape does nothing** — round 1 alone suggested exactly that, and round 2
refuted it.

⚠️ **That is a property of THIS inference stack, not of embeddings in general.**
The same quantity measured ~1.2e-3 on OpenRouter/text-embedding-3-large — ~10×
larger, and large enough to matter. A constant measured on one serving stack does
not transfer to another; that is the whole reason this script exists rather than a
number written into a comment. (Corrected 2026-08-02 after I had done exactly
that: `memo-verify-provenance` briefly carried the OpenRouter figure as though it
described this endpoint.)

⚑ Singleton re-embedding remains the verifier's deliberate choice: it is the
worst-case call shape against a batched writer, so a pass is conservative rather
than flattering.

    docker compose exec -T memo-v2 python3 /app/scripts/memo-calibrate-drift
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import asyncio
import math
import struct
import sys
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1] / "src"))
sys.path.insert(0, "/app/src")

from memo import db, embeddings  # noqa: E402
from memo.config import settings  # noqa: E402

# Matches the batch size the corpus was WRITTEN with. Changing this measures a
# different question than the one the verifier faces.
WRITE_BATCH = 16
CONTROL_MAX = 0.999


def cos(a, b) -> float:
    dot = sum(x * y for x, y in zip(a, b))
    na = math.sqrt(sum(x * x for x in a))
    nb = math.sqrt(sum(y * y for y in b))
    return dot / (na * nb) if na and nb else 0.0


async def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    ap.add_argument("--sample", type=int, default=WRITE_BATCH,
                    help="chunks to draw; also the batch size used for A and C")
    # Not a confidence knob — a REFUTATION knob. One run of a nondeterministic
    # quantity can legitimately return 0.000000 and support the wrong conclusion.
    ap.add_argument("--repeat", type=int, default=3,
                    help="independent measurement rounds; the floor reported is "
                         "the worst across all of them")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    conn = db._get_or_create_conn(db.global_path())
    rows = conn.execute(
        "SELECT doc_id, chunk_index, text FROM document_chunks ORDER BY created_at"
    ).fetchall()
    if len(rows) < 2:
        print("need at least 2 indexed chunks to calibrate (the control needs a "
              "second, unrelated chunk)", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    total = len(rows)
    step = max(1, total // args.sample)
    rows = rows[::step][:args.sample]

    print(f"model      : {settings.embedding_model} @ {settings.embedding_dimensions}")
    print(f"sampled    : {len(rows)} of {total} chunks, stride {step} "
          f"(stratified by created_at)")
    print(f"batch size : {len(rows)}")

    print(f"repeats    : {args.repeat} (floor reported is the worst across all)")
    texts = [r[2] for r in rows]

    stored = []
    for doc_id, idx, _ in rows:
        blob = conn.execute(
            "SELECT embedding FROM chunk_embeddings WHERE doc_id=? AND chunk_index=?",
            (doc_id, idx)).fetchone()
        if not blob:
            print(f"{doc_id[:8]}#{idx}: chunk row with no vector — the index is "
                  f"inconsistent", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        stored.append(struct.unpack(f"{len(blob[0]) // 4}f", blob[0]))

    comp: list[float] = []
    dnow: list[float] = []
    dbat: list[float] = []
    control_worst = 0.0

    for r in range(args.repeat):
        # Serially, one request each — this IS the call shape the verifier uses.
        singles = [await embeddings.embed(t) for t in texts]

        batched = await embeddings.embed_batch(texts)
        if len(batched) != len(texts):
            # A short or reordered batch response pairs vectors to the WRONG chunks
            # and manufactures a drift figure out of its own plumbing. Refuse rather
            # than report.
            print(f"batch returned {len(batched)} vectors for {len(texts)} inputs — "
                  f"cannot pair; refusing to report a number", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

        ra, rb, rc = [], [], []
        moved = []
        for i, (doc_id, idx, _) in enumerate(rows):
            a, b, c = (cos(singles[i], batched[i]),
                       cos(singles[i], stored[i]),
                       cos(batched[i], stored[i]))
            ra.append(a); rb.append(b); rc.append(c)
            if min(a, b, c) < 1.0 - 1e-9:
                moved.append(f"{doc_id[:8]}#{idx} "
                             f"A={a:.8f} B={b:.8f} C={c:.8f}")

        comp += ra; dnow += rb; dbat += rc

        # THE CONTROL, every round. Without it a stack that returns ~1.0 to
        # everything produces a full column of clean ticks and reads as a tight,
        # healthy floor.
        control = cos(singles[0], singles[-1])
        control_worst = max(control_worst, control)

        print(f"\nround {r + 1}/{args.repeat}: "
              f"A {1 - min(ra):.2e}  B {1 - min(rb):.2e}  C {1 - min(rc):.2e}   "
              f"control {control:.6f}   ({len(moved)} of {len(rows)} rows moved)")
        for line in moved:
            print(f"    {line}")

    if control_worst >= CONTROL_MAX:
        print("CONTROL FAILED — the comparison returns ~1.0 to unrelated text, so "
              "every figure above is meaningless. The instrument is broken, not "
              "the corpus.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    def rep(name, xs):
        worst = min(xs)
        print(f"  {name:<15} worst {worst:.8f}   (1 - worst = {1 - worst:.2e})")

    print(f"\nacross {args.repeat} rounds × {len(rows)} chunks:")
    rep("A composition", comp)
    rep("B drift-now", dnow)
    rep("C drift-batch", dbat)

    floor = min(min(dnow), min(dbat))
    margin = (1 - CONTROL_MAX) / max(1 - floor, 1e-12)
    print(f"\nThe threshold must clear the worst of B and C — those are what the "
          f"verifier actually computes.\n0.999 clears {floor:.8f} by {margin:.1f}×.")
    if margin < 2:
        print("MARGIN TOO THIN — a healthy corpus is within 2× of tripping the "
              "provenance check. Raise the threshold or investigate what moved in "
              "the serving environment.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    rc = asyncio.run(main())
    print(f"EXIT={rc}", flush=True)
    raise SystemExit(rc)
